WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.876
<v Human Services Supervisor>I lost two experienced specialists this year. Both said pay

00:00:03.876 --> 00:00:07.751
<v Human Services Supervisor>in their exit conversations. I am thinking the retention plan

00:00:07.751 --> 00:00:10.464
<v Human Services Supervisor>should be a request for higher classifications.

00:00:10.814 --> 00:00:15.205
<v Program Manager>Pay is real, and it does predict leaving. But in

00:00:15.205 --> 00:00:19.597
<v Program Manager>the biggest review of turnover research, covering 316 articles, pay

00:00:19.597 --> 00:00:23.988
<v Program Manager>was one of the weaker predictors. Job satisfaction, commitment, leadership

00:00:23.988 --> 00:00:27.062
<v Program Manager>and something called embeddedness each mattered more.

00:00:27.412 --> 00:00:30.748
<v Human Services Supervisor>Embeddedness. What does that mean in practice?

00:00:31.098 --> 00:00:34.508
<v Program Manager>It is the web that holds someone in the job.

00:00:34.508 --> 00:00:37.918
<v Program Manager>Links to colleagues and partners. Fit between the work and

00:00:37.918 --> 00:00:41.328
<v Program Manager>their skills. And what they would give up by leaving.

00:00:41.328 --> 00:00:44.738
<v Program Manager>One review of about 43,000 people found it predicted staying

00:00:44.738 --> 00:00:47.466
<v Program Manager>even after accounting for how satisfied people were.

00:00:47.816 --> 00:00:51.096
<v Human Services Supervisor>So two people could be equally satisfied, and the one

00:00:51.096 --> 00:00:53.720
<v Human Services Supervisor>with more ties is more likely to stay.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.881
<v Program Manager>That is the idea. And notice what happened in your

00:00:56.881 --> 00:00:59.693
<v Program Manager>unit. One of the people who left had a review

00:00:59.693 --> 00:01:01.942
<v Program Manager>partner who left earlier. Did that come up?

00:01:02.292 --> 00:01:05.563
<v Human Services Supervisor>Not in the exit conversation. But now that you mention

00:01:05.563 --> 00:01:08.834
<v Human Services Supervisor>it, they did seem more isolated after that. And I

00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:12.105
<v Human Services Supervisor>have a strong specialist right now who has gone quiet.

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.740
<v Human Services Supervisor>They train every new hire.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.210
<v Program Manager>Then do not wait for an exit conversation. Exit answers

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.330
<v Program Manager>come at a moment when people have little reason to

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.450
<v Program Manager>say everything. Pay is easy to name. Talk with your

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.946
<v Program Manager>specialist now, while there is time to act.

00:01:26.296 --> 00:01:28.627
<v Human Services Supervisor>What do I ask? I do not want it to

00:01:28.627 --> 00:01:30.959
<v Human Services Supervisor>sound like I am trying to talk them out of

00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:31.192
<v Human Services Supervisor>something.

00:01:31.542 --> 00:01:34.474
<v Program Manager>Make it a regular conversation you have with everyone. Ask

00:01:34.474 --> 00:01:37.406
<v Program Manager>what they look forward to, who they rely on, and

00:01:37.406 --> 00:01:40.338
<v Program Manager>what would make the next year harder. Then pick one

00:01:40.338 --> 00:01:42.390
<v Program Manager>thing you control and give a date.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.612
<v Human Services Supervisor>And if they bring up pay?

00:01:44.962 --> 00:01:48.562
<v Program Manager>Be honest about what you can and cannot change. Raise

00:01:48.562 --> 00:01:52.162
<v Program Manager>a real classification question with the people responsible. Then look

00:01:52.162 --> 00:01:55.762
<v Program Manager>at load, support and growth. In the child welfare research,

00:01:55.762 --> 00:01:59.362
<v Program Manager>stress, burnout and lack of support mattered more than who

00:01:59.362 --> 00:02:02.962
<v Program Manager>the worker was. Those are things a supervisor can shape.

00:02:02.962 --> 00:02:06.202
<v Program Manager>None of it is guaranteed. It shifts the odds.
